    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zboriv">Treaty of Zboriv</a> of 18 August 1649 recognised the Hetmanate within the Commonwealth, with 40,000 Registered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and Crown forces barred from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, Bratslav and Chernihiv lands.</li><li>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> Council of 1654 Khmelnytsky and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> elite swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexis in return for self-government, a bond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> tightened with every later agreement.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a>'s troops burnt the capital Baturyn on 13 November 1708, killing up to 15,000 people, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Ivan Mazepa</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Visitors found the Hetmanate unusually literate: in the 1740s 866 of 1,099 settlements in seven regimental districts had primary schools, more per head than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and Paul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> wrote in 1654 that even wives and daughters could read.</p></aside>
    </section>

