    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky.jpg" alt="Bohdan Khmelnytsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bohdan_Khmelnytsky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cossack Hetmanate, officially the Zaporozhian Host, was a stratocratic state built by Registered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> of 1648 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. After victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity, and in February 1649 told a Polish delegation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> that he meant to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> of a Ruthenia stretching to Chelm and Halych.</p><p>That summer he besieged Jeremi Wiśniowiecki at Zbarazh and, with İslâm III Giray's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, ambushed King John II Casimir near Zboriv; the king won over the khan, and Khmelnytsky had to negotiate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zboriv">Treaty of Zboriv</a> of 18 August 1649 raised the Registered Cossacks to 40,000 and barred Crown Forces from the Kyiv, Bratslav and Chernihiv voivodeships. From this base Khmelnytsky built a state in military, administrative, financial, economic and cultural spheres, investing the Host under its hetman with supreme power and forming an elite of Cossack officers and Ruthenian nobles. The Hetmanate used Polish currency and often Polish as its language of administration until, after 1667, the “simple language” of Ukraine entered its documents. Its people called the land Ukraine and themselves the Ruthenian nation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> called it Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> “the country of Ukraine”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/folwark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/folwark.jpg" alt="Folwark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/folwark">Folwark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bremervoerde-1653-Merian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthäus Merian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy stayed agrarian. The uprising expropriated the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, nobles and tenants: their lands, livestock and property passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, peasants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and the state, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/folwark">folwark</a> manor system and Catholic Church holdings were eliminated after the Battle of Batih in 1652, and the peasants became free. After the uprising 80–90 per cent of peasants held land; roughly half the territory was officers' grants or free self-governing villages, a third officers' and nobles' land, and 17 per cent the Church's. The starshyna enlarged its estates by purchase, often forced, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetmans</a>' grants and tsarist awards, until more than 35 per cent of cultivated land was its private property by the 1730s.</p><p>Society had five estates: nobility, Cossacks, clergy, townspeople and peasants. Cossacks were about 45 per cent of the population in the 1760s; after 1735 only Elected Cossacks kept their privileges and served in person, while Helper Cossacks supplied the army. Tax-exempt monasteries owned over 20 per cent of households in nine regiments in 1729–1730. The Military Treasury drew on taxes on mills and breweries, redemptions for horilka, tar and tobacco, beehives, customs and land rent. Distilling flourished in every village, with more than 10,000 distilleries by the late eighteenth century, and saltpetre works supplied the Russian treasury.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava.jpg" alt="Battle of Poltava" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marten%27s_Poltava.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre-Denis Martin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hetmanate was an army with a territory. Its land was divided into regiments, polky, each under a colonel who was both military and civil authority, subdivided into sotnias and kurins. The core was the Registered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, free soldiers who armed and horsed themselves. The Zaporozhian Sich on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> kept a distinct status, a refuge for Cossacks fleeing the Hetmanate and often its opponent.</p><p>Khmelnytsky's wars with the Commonwealth depended on Crimean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> cavalry, allies who betrayed the Cossacks three times by 1653. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> a joint Cossack–Muscovite army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1654, took White Ruthenia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, and Khmelnytsky halted a Polish–Tatar counterstroke at the Battle of Okhmativ in 1655. The Ruin of 1657–1687 was civil war: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vyhovsky">Ivan Vyhovsky</a> crushed Martyn Pushkar's revolt near Poltava in 1658 with Tatar help and routed a Muscovite army at Konotop in 1659; Petro Doroshenko fought the Commonwealth with Ottoman and Tatar support, and the Ottoman–Tatar army destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a> in the Russo-Turkish War that followed his fall in 1676. When Mazepa joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> on 28 October 1708, Peter I had Menshikov burn Baturyn, killing up to 15,000 people on 13 November, executed over 900 officers at Lebedyn, destroyed the Zaporozhian Sich in May 1709 and beat the Swedes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a> on 8 July 1709 with Skoropadsky's Cossacks in his ranks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pereiaslav.jpg" alt="Pereiaslav" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%80_(%D0%BC%D1%83%D1%80.)_%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%8F%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2-%D0%A5%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kiyanka, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Supreme power belonged in principle to the General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Council of all Cossacks, townspeople, clergy and even peasants, which chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>; by the late seventeenth century the Council of Officers, the starshyna, did the choosing, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> the tsar had to confirm every nomination. The hetman held complete power over administration, judiciary, finances and army, and conducted foreign policy until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> stripped that right away in the eighteenth century. Colonels, first elected by their regiments, were appointed by the hetman and after 1709 often by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p>The relation to Moscow was set by treaties: the March Articles of 1654, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> Articles of 1659 that opened the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hlukhiv">Hlukhiv</a> Articles of 1669 and the Konotop Articles of 1672, which removed the right to an independent foreign policy and took the election of the hetman from ordinary Cossacks. Peter I created the Little Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegium-russia">Collegium</a> in 1722, six Russian officers under Stepan Velyaminov answering to the Senate, a parallel government; acting hetman Pavlo Polubotok died in the Peter and Paul Fortress in 1724. Danylo Apostol's 28 Authoritative Ordinances of 1728 allowed ten regiments, a mixed court and a law code in force into the nineteenth century. In exile, Pylyp Orlyk's Pacts and Constitutions of 1710 gave the Cossack state a democratic frame.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kyiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyiv.jpg" alt="Kyiv" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyiv_(234807751).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Anzola, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For the first time a major part of the ethnic Ukrainian lands was united under one rule in a de facto independent polity, and that fact outlasted the state. Khmelnytsky's multi-vectored diplomacy, weighing an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>–Protestant bloc, a third partner's place in the Commonwealth or the Ottoman orbit, drew the Tsardom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the Porte into the Ukrainian question. The Treaty of Hadiach of 1658 proposed a Grand Duchy of Ruthenia equal to Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>; Doroshenko's submission to the sultan made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> an Ottoman sanjak on paper, though the Porte never imposed Islamic tribute and preferred a buffer.</p><p>Culturally it flowered under Mazepa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> collegium became an academy that drew the leading scholars of the Orthodox world, the largest school in lands ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; a collegium opened in Chernihiv and a musical academy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hlukhiv">Hlukhiv</a> in 1730 trained Maksym Berezovsky and Dmitry Bortniansky. Mazepa financed the Church of the Epiphany, St Michael's Golden-Domed cathedral and the restoration of Saint Sophia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style. The starshyna's descendants, proving their nobility from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetmans</a>' treaties after 1785, revived interest in the Cossack past, and the image of a free Cossack Ukraine against despotic Muscovy, spread by the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, inspired the Ukrainian national revival of the nineteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kyrylo-rozumovsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyrylo-rozumovsky.jpg" alt="Kyrylo Rozumovsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kyrylo-rozumovsky">Kyrylo Rozumovsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirill_Razumovsky_Tokke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis Tocqué, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hetmanate was dismantled by stages. The Treaty of Andrusovo of 1667, made without any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> representative, split it along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> abolished Cossack self-government on the Right Bank in 1699. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> autonomy became nominal: the territory went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, three imperial regiments were stationed permanently, Peter I's trade rules banned exports and redirected trade through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the Little Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegium-russia">Collegium</a> ruled from 1722 to 1727. After Danylo Apostol's hetmanship (1727–1734) a Governing Council dominated by Russian officers ruled.</p><p>Elizabeth, secretly married to the Cossack Oleksiy Rozumovsky, heard the starshyna's petition for their “ancient liberties” in 1744 and in 1750 had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrylo-rozumovsky">Kyrylo Rozumovsky</a> “elected” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> in absentia. He moved the capital back to Baturyn, reformed the courts in 1760–1763 and convened starshyna congresses. A congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hlukhiv">Hlukhiv</a> proposing a hereditary hetmanship and a return to Khmelnytsky's terms alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. Rozumovsky was summoned in January 1764 and forced to resign; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>, advised by Grigory Teplov that the Hetmanate's “republican” law was incompatible with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>, abolished the office by decree of 21 October 1764 and gave power to a second Little Russian Collegium under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-rumyantsev">Pyotr Rumyantsev</a>. The Zaporozhian Sich was liquidated in 1775, the regiments on 27 September 1781, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> followed in 1783.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Cossack Hetmanate, born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>'s uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and recognised at Zboriv in 1649, placed itself under the tsar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a> in 1654, was partitioned along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> in 1667 and, after Mazepa's failed break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1708–1709, lost its autonomy piece by piece until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> abolished the hetmanship in 1764.</em></p>

